From: Ahrion Gallegos <ahrionmgallegos@gmail.com>
Subject: net: gro_cells: opt out of threaded NAPI

Writing 1 to /sys/class/net/<dev>/threaded on a gro_cells device corrupts
the receive path. This makes those NAPIs decline to be threaded, so the
write becomes inert on them instead: it still threads whatever NAPIs the
driver registered itself, and leaves the gro_cells ones in softirq
context where they are safe.

gro_cells (net/core/gro_cells.c) carries no lock at 6.12.103. The
producer takes the running CPU's cell at :28 with this_cpu_ptr() and
enqueues at :38 with the unlocked __skb_queue_tail(); gro_cell_poll()
dequeues at :58 with the unlocked __skb_dequeue(). Neither takes
napi_skbs.lock. So the entire mutual exclusion between producer and
consumer is that both run on one CPU with BH disabled, which is what the
"called under BH context" comment at :50 is asserting. Sebastian Andrzej
Siewior states the same invariant in 25718fdcbdd2 ("net: gro_cells: Use
nested-BH locking for gro_cell", v6.18): "The gro_cell data structure is
per-CPU variable and relies on disabled BH for its locking." That commit
adds a local_lock_t for lockdep and PREEMPT_RT and says it "does not
alter the functional behaviour for !PREEMPT_RT", so it is not a fix for
this, and backporting it would change nothing here.

Threading breaks the invariant because the kthread is not bound.
gro_cells_init() puts its per-CPU NAPIs on the real netdev's
dev->napi_list with netif_napi_add() at :85. dev_set_threaded()
(net/core/dev.c:6688) then walks that list with no filter of any kind,
and napi_kthread_create() (:1500) starts each thread with a plain
kthread_run(). One CPU's cell can therefore be drained by a kthread the
scheduler placed on another, mid-enqueue: an unlocked __skb_queue_tail()
racing an unlocked __skb_dequeue() on the same sk_buff_head from two
cores. The NAPI SCHED bit serialises pollers against each other; it
never serialises a poller against the producer, because the producer
does not take it.

Nor does the work fall back to softirq once the bit is set.
____napi_schedule() (:4637) takes the threaded arm, calls
wake_up_process() and returns without ever reaching sd->poll_list, so no
ksoftirqd path is left to catch it. The one carve-out there, at :4653,
is for the backlog NAPI -- and that one is CPU-bound: backlog_napi_setup()
(:12282) does not create a thread at all, it adopts one from the smpboot
per-CPU pool registered at :12356, and kernel/smpboot.c:184 creates those
with kthread_create_on_cpu(). So the kernel already treats "per-CPU NAPI"
and "unbound kthread" as incompatible for its own; it just has no way for
anyone else to say so.

The mechanism:

  * NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD, a new state bit appended to the enum so no
    existing bit is renumbered. gro_cells_init() sets it next to the
    NAPI_STATE_NO_BUSY_POLL it already sets, before netif_napi_add(),
    because that call can create the kthread there and then on a device
    whose threaded flag is already on.
  * dev_set_threaded() skips such NAPIs in both of its loops -- it
    creates no kthread for them, and never assigns NAPI_STATE_THREADED
    to them. Skipping the second loop in both directions is safe
    precisely because the bit is never set, so there is never one left
    to clear.
  * netif_napi_add_weight() (:6765) honours it too. That is the third
    site, and leaving it out would have left a live hole: a NAPI added
    to an already-threaded device gets its kthread there rather than
    from dev_set_threaded().

Those three cover every path. napi_enable() (:6835) is the fourth and
last place NAPI_STATE_THREADED can be set, and its condition at :6843 is
"n->dev->threaded && n->thread" -- with no kthread ever created,
n->thread stays NULL and the bit cannot be set that way either. The two
remaining references, at :6266 and :11818, are "napi->state &=
NAPIF_STATE_THREADED" reached only under napi->poll == process_backlog,
so they cannot clear NO_THREAD on a gro_cell.

What the sysfs file means afterwards: on a device whose every NAPI opts
out, writing 1 succeeds, reads back 1, and threads nothing -- which is
exactly what the file already does today on a device with no NAPI at
all, so no userspace expectation changes. I considered returning
-EOPNOTSUPP in that case so the write would fail visibly, and did not:
it would be an ABI change inside a patch whose job is to stop a
corruption, and it would give a mixed device two different answers
depending on which NAPIs happen to exist when the write lands.

This is not specific to this board or to my 991. Every gro_cells user at
6.12.103 -- amt, bareudp, geneve, macsec, pfcp, vxlan, ip_tunnel and
rmnet_vnd -- exposes the same writable sysfs file with the same exposure
on any non-RT kernel. rmnet_vnd is Qualcomm's modem netdev in mainline,
so the hazard is reachable on stock hardware by writing one file, with
no local patch applied first.

I hit it here because 991 moved wwan0 into that class: stock
mhi_wwan_mbim registers no NAPI at all (a bare netif_rx() at
mhi_wwan_mbim.c:348), so the toggle was inert before. Writing 1 to it
took the WAN down twice on 2026-09-14, silently, with no recovery from
writing 0 and a reboot needed each time. That is consistent with list
corruption rather than with an overflow, but I am not grading the fit
above reasoning: the case against threading here is the source read
above, not those two outages. x3000/docs/xdp-methods-tested.md section
23.21 carries the full record, including what the outages do and do not
establish.

For submission this splits in two, in the usual order: one patch adding
NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD and teaching dev_set_threaded() and
netif_napi_add_weight() to honour it, then one patch setting it in
gro_cells_init(). Kept as a single patch here because the first half is
dead code without the second.

Independent of 991, 992, 993 and 995: it touches core net only, no
driver, and shares no file with them, so the apply order between them
does not matter. Numbered 996 because 994 is retired -- see 995's
message and xdp-methods-tested.md section 22.7.

Out-of-tree patch for the GL-X3000 lean build.

--- a/include/linux/netdevice.h
+++ b/include/linux/netdevice.h
@@ -392,6 +392,7 @@ enum {
 	NAPI_STATE_PREFER_BUSY_POLL,	/* prefer busy-polling over softirq processing*/
 	NAPI_STATE_THREADED,		/* The poll is performed inside its own thread*/
 	NAPI_STATE_SCHED_THREADED,	/* Napi is currently scheduled in threaded mode */
+	NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD,		/* Poll must run on the scheduling CPU */
 };
 
 enum {
@@ -405,6 +406,7 @@ enum {
 	NAPIF_STATE_PREFER_BUSY_POLL	= BIT(NAPI_STATE_PREFER_BUSY_POLL),
 	NAPIF_STATE_THREADED		= BIT(NAPI_STATE_THREADED),
 	NAPIF_STATE_SCHED_THREADED	= BIT(NAPI_STATE_SCHED_THREADED),
+	NAPIF_STATE_NO_THREAD		= BIT(NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD),
 };
 
 enum gro_result {
--- a/net/core/dev.c
+++ b/net/core/dev.c
@@ -6695,6 +6695,8 @@ int dev_set_threaded(struct net_device *
 
 	if (threaded) {
 		list_for_each_entry(napi, &dev->napi_list, dev_list) {
+			if (test_bit(NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD, &napi->state))
+				continue;
 			if (!napi->thread) {
 				err = napi_kthread_create(napi);
 				if (err) {
@@ -6718,8 +6720,18 @@ int dev_set_threaded(struct net_device *
 	 * softirq mode will happen in the next round of napi_schedule().
 	 * This should not cause hiccups/stalls to the live traffic.
 	 */
-	list_for_each_entry(napi, &dev->napi_list, dev_list)
+	list_for_each_entry(napi, &dev->napi_list, dev_list) {
+		/* A NAPI that must poll on the CPU which scheduled it is
+		 * skipped in both directions: the bit is never set on it, so
+		 * there is never one to clear. Writing the sysfs file on a
+		 * device whose every NAPI opts out therefore succeeds and
+		 * changes nothing, exactly as it does today on a device with
+		 * no NAPI at all.
+		 */
+		if (test_bit(NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD, &napi->state))
+			continue;
 		assign_bit(NAPI_STATE_THREADED, &napi->state, threaded);
+	}
 
 	return err;
 }
@@ -6794,8 +6806,14 @@ void netif_napi_add_weight(struct net_de
 	/* Create kthread for this napi if dev->threaded is set.
 	 * Clear dev->threaded if kthread creation failed so that
 	 * threaded mode will not be enabled in napi_enable().
+	 *
+	 * NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD is honoured here as well as in
+	 * dev_set_threaded(): a NAPI added to an already-threaded device
+	 * takes this path instead, and leaving napi->thread NULL is what
+	 * keeps napi_enable() from setting NAPI_STATE_THREADED later.
 	 */
-	if (dev->threaded && napi_kthread_create(napi))
+	if (dev->threaded && !test_bit(NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD, &napi->state) &&
+	    napi_kthread_create(napi))
 		dev->threaded = false;
 	netif_napi_set_irq(napi, -1);
 }
--- a/net/core/gro_cells.c
+++ b/net/core/gro_cells.c
@@ -82,6 +82,23 @@ int gro_cells_init(struct gro_cells *gce
 
 		set_bit(NAPI_STATE_NO_BUSY_POLL, &cell->napi.state);
 
+		/* These NAPIs must poll on the CPU that scheduled them.
+		 * gro_cells_receive() enqueues into this_cpu_ptr()'s cell with
+		 * the unlocked __skb_queue_tail() and gro_cell_poll() dequeues
+		 * with the unlocked __skb_dequeue(), so the whole mutual
+		 * exclusion between producer and consumer is that both run on
+		 * one CPU with BH disabled -- the contract the "called under BH
+		 * context" comment above gro_cell_poll() states. An unbound
+		 * kthread breaks it, and napi_kthread_create() uses a plain
+		 * kthread_run(), so without this bit dev_set_threaded() would
+		 * let one CPU's cell be drained from another mid-enqueue.
+		 *
+		 * Set before netif_napi_add() because that adds the NAPI to
+		 * dev->napi_list and may create the kthread there and then, on
+		 * a device that is already threaded.
+		 */
+		set_bit(NAPI_STATE_NO_THREAD, &cell->napi.state);
+
 		netif_napi_add(dev, &cell->napi, gro_cell_poll);
 		napi_enable(&cell->napi);
 	}
